      "card_ids": ["C13"],
      "claim": "DAPPD, a 2,6-diaminopurine nucleoside analogue, was detected in plasma, CSF, and brain after IP and oral dosing in mice, directly demonstrating BBB permeability for a close purine analogue.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C14"],
      "claim": "Nelarabine showed excellent CSF penetration in non-human primate pharmacokinetic studies, supporting CNS access for purine nucleoside analogues.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C10"],
      "claim": "Cladribine reaches CSF concentrations approximately 25% of plasma in humans, providing direct human CSF/plasma evidence for a lipophilic purine nucleoside.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C06"],
      "claim": "Fludarabine distributes into mouse brain with uptake about 2.5-fold higher than cladribine/clofarabine and is transporter-mediated, showing that purine nucleoside analogues can achieve meaningful brain exposure.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C07"],
      "claim": "DDA had a brain uptake index below 2% in rats, indicating poor BBB transport for a dideoxyadenosine analogue; this is less transferable to the carbocyclic 2,6-diaminopurine query and is outweighed by positive nucleoside evidence.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "Pass. The query is abacavir, a carbocyclic purine nucleoside analogue. Multiple structurally related purine nucleosides show direct BBB/CSF penetration (DAPPD, nelarabine, cladribine, fludarabine), while the negative BUI/in situ results come from less transferable dideoxyadenosine/adenosine analogues. Overall evidence supports meaningful CNS access.",
